# Refract — Controls

This file defines how a player operates the game: the pointer draws beams on the
board, and the keyboard clears the board and drives the menus. Which segments the
rules permit is in `specs/beams.md`, and the cell centers the pointer is measured
against are in `specs/board.md`. Every figure below carries the name this
specification gives it.

## The pointer

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
This game stands on no engine, so the pointer belongs to the runtime layer you
write. What the game reads from that layer is a pointer position in the game's
logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`) stage, a press edge, and
a release edge. How the layer produces those three is yours to decide.
{{else}}
The pointer belongs to the engine, which documents it in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it is a
pointer position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H`
(`1280 x 720`) stage, a press edge, and a release edge.
{{/if}}

The game acts on those three alone, and this file states what it does with them.
Each pointer position the game reads is resolved on its own, in the order the
positions arrive, so a beam grows and unwinds one node at a time as the pointer
travels.

Menu navigation runs through the actions listed at the end of this file.

## Targeting a node

The pointer targets the node whose cell center lies within `NODE_HIT_R` (`44`) of
the pointer position. `NODE_HIT_R` is below half of `CELL_PITCH` (`96`), which is
`48`, so no two targeting regions overlap and at most one node is ever targeted.
The pointer targets no node when it is farther than `NODE_HIT_R` from every cell
center, and an empty cell holds no node to target.

## Beginning a trace

A trace runs from a press to the matching release. A press that targets a node
begins one according to that node's place on the board. The rows below are
**evaluated in order**, and the first row that matches is the one that applies.

| Targeted node | What the press does |
| --- | --- |
| An emitter of a channel whose beam carries no segments | Starts that channel's beam at that emitter. |
| Either end of a channel's beam | Resumes that beam from that end. |
| A node exactly one beam passes through | Shortens that beam to end at that node, and resumes it from there. |

A press that matches no row begins no trace and leaves the board unchanged. That
covers a press on an empty cell, a press off the board, a press on a node that
carries no beam and is not an emitter of a channel whose beam carries no
segments, and a press on a node that **more than one beam** passes through. A
node two beams share is edited by grabbing one of those beams at one of its ends
or at a node no other beam uses.

The trace carries the channel of the beam it began, and the node it began at is
the live end.

## The drawn order and shortening

A beam's cells are held in the order they were drawn, starting from the node the
trace that created the beam began at. Whenever a trace begins on a beam, the beam
is first oriented so that the node the trace extends from is its **last** cell,
reversing the held order when the trace begins from the other end. The live end
is therefore always the beam's last cell.

Shortening drops every cell after the targeted node in that order, leaves the
rest of the beam drawn, and resumes the trace from the targeted node. A beam that
crosses a node more than once carries that node more than once, and the last of
those occurrences in the held order is the one kept, so the beam loses as few
segments as it can.

## Extending

While the trace is held, the pointer moving within `NODE_HIT_R` of a node
adjacent to the live end adds the segment joining them, when the rules in
`specs/beams.md` permit that segment. That node becomes the new live end.

A move those rules refuse changes nothing and the trace stays live, so a player
can sweep the pointer across the board and draw only permitted segments. A move
into a crystal whose charges are all spent is one of the refused moves: entering
a crystal spends a charge, and that crystal has none left to spend.

## Retracting

The pointer moving within `NODE_HIT_R` of the node immediately behind the live
end removes the last segment, and that node becomes the live end. A beam is
unwound one segment at a time by backing the pointer along it without releasing.

## Releasing

The release edge ends the trace and leaves the beam exactly as drawn, complete or
not. A partial beam persists on the board, and a later press on either of its
ends resumes it. A trace that added no segment leaves its channel's beam carrying
none, so a later press on either of that channel's emitters starts the beam
afresh.

A trace also ends the moment the board is solved. On the frame a segment
satisfies the solved condition the trace ends and the beams stay exactly as
drawn, and the release edge that follows begins nothing and changes nothing.

## Clearing

The `clear` action empties every beam on the board at once and leaves the board's
nodes untouched, so a player can start a board over without leaving it. It is
read on the `playing` screen and does nothing on any other screen.

## Actions and bindings

`ACTIONS` holds the actions the game registers with the runtime, and `BINDINGS`
maps each action to the keys that fire it. Each action is read once per frame as
a press edge.

| Action | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | Moves the menu highlight up. |
| `down` | Moves the menu highlight down. |
| `left` | Moves the menu highlight left. |
| `right` | Moves the menu highlight right. |
| `confirm` | Accepts the highlighted item. |
| `back` | Leaves the current screen. |
| `clear` | Empties every beam on the board. |
| `mute` | Toggles sound, from any screen. |

`clear` is bound to `KeyR`. The remaining bindings are yours, and each action
carries the keys a player reaches for by habit. The screens these actions move
between, and where each one leads, are in `specs/ui.md`,
`specs/modes/campaign.md`, and `specs/modes/cascade.md`.
